<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhilala</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhilala are a tribal community of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhils</a>. Tradition traces them to unions between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> and Bhils, and they often formed a landholding and village-headman class among the Bhils. They are recognised as a Scheduled Tribe in modern India.</p></div>
